    "description": "Zard Kuh is a mountain chain in the central Zagros of Chaharmahal and Bakhtiari Province. Its high slopes preserve evidence of glaciation within a wider landscape shaped by seasonal snow, deeply cut valleys, and highland land use.",
    "tags": ["Zard Kuh", "Zagros", "Bakhtiari highlands", "Glaciers", "Snow", "Mountain landscapes"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:40:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:40:07Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Zard Kuh: Snow and Ice in the Central Zagros | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Zard Kuh’s glacier research, seasonal snow, and place within the folded ridges and highland landscapes of the Bakhtiari Zagros."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "regional-setting",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A mountain chain in the Bakhtiari Zagros",
        "content": "Zard Kuh lies in the central Zagros, within Chaharmahal and Bakhtiari Province. The [2009 glacier inventory of Iran](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/annals-of-glaciology/article/new-glacier-inventory-of-iran/18BCB89BEEBD29F27C78C0C2C4BAE36C) treats it as a mountain chain and a distinct glacier region. This broader landscape is more informative than a summit ranking alone: snow and ice occupy particular slopes and hollows across the mountains. [s1]\n\nThe surrounding Bakhtiari highlands consist of folded ridges, intervening basins, and deeply incised valleys. The [Encyclopaedia Iranica regional account](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/baktiari-mountains-of-the-zagros-range/) explains how transverse gorges cut through the prevailing ridge pattern. The result is a landscape in which drainage crosses as well as follows the structural grain of the mountains. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "snow-and-glaciation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why ice can persist here",
        "content": "Seasonality is central to Zard Kuh’s physical geography. The US Geological Survey’s [Glaciers of Iran chapter](https://parstimes.com/spaceimages/iran-glaciers.pdf) describes dry summers and precipitation during the cooler seasons in the Zagros. Elevation allows precipitation to accumulate as snow, while local exposure helps explain why ice survives in selected places. [s3]\n\nThe chapter recounts observations made on Zard Kuh’s northern slopes in August 1933 and subsequent research in 1975. These investigations documented small glaciers and persistent snow. Their value is historical: they establish that such features were studied at particular dates, without establishing their present boundaries. [s3]\n\nAn [Iranica review of glaciers](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/glaciers/) likewise describes small cirque glaciers and firn patches around Zard Kuh. A cirque is a bowl-shaped mountain hollow; firn is compacted snow that has survived beyond a single melt season. The review emphasizes the favorable local conditions required for ice to persist near the upper limits of the massif. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "highland-land-use",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The wider inhabited highlands",
        "content": "Zard Kuh belongs to a region where mountain basins, river terraces, and upland pastures support different forms of land use. Iranica’s historical regional synthesis describes cultivation where irrigation is possible and seasonal grazing on higher slopes. It also places oak woodland within the ecological history of the Bakhtiari mountains. These are regional patterns, not a claim that the summit zone itself is wooded or cultivated. [s2]\n\nThis distinction helps connect the mountain to its surroundings. The high slopes are part of a lived landscape extending down through grazing country to cultivated valleys, rather than a separate wilderness detached from neighboring communities. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-record",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the mountain’s evidence carefully",
        "content": "The historical glacier record contains different inventories and observation dates. A count or area from one investigation cannot automatically be compared with another: researchers may map different boundaries or distinguish snow, exposed ice, and debris-covered ice differently. The USGS chapter’s chronology is therefore more defensible here than an apparently continuous numerical trend. [s3]\n\nZard Kuh’s importance lies partly in this combination of features: a snowy high mountain environment within the Zagros, localized surviving ice, and a wider inhabited highland region. Establishing how much ice remains requires dated observations with consistent mapping, rather than treating older descriptions as current measurements. [s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
